            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Conclusion First: High Reach Doesn't Equal High Sales</h2>
<p>When reviewing Meta ad creatives, most people immediately check impressions, click-through rate (CTR), and cost per click. If this is your review order, you're likely still thinking in "traffic logic" rather than "conversion logic."</p>
<p><strong>High reach</strong> means the creative gets impressions, clicks, and keeps the ad running. <strong>High sales</strong> means the creative drives effective conversions, clear ROAS, and sustainable ad spend. These two outcomes have no inherent correlation. A creative with 5% CTR but 0.5% conversion rate is far less valuable than one with 2% CTR and 3% conversion rate.</p>
<p>Here are three key dimensions for determining whether a creative "runs well" or "sells well."</p>
<h2>Dimension 1: CTR vs. CVR — Don't Focus on One Side Only</h2>
<p>High CTR doesn't guarantee conversions. A cross-border e-commerce team tested two casual game ads: Creative A had 6.2% CTR with 0.3% conversion rate and $28 CPA; Creative B had 2.1% CTR with 2.8% conversion rate and $9 CPA. Same budget, but the latter achieved positive ROAS while the former was paused after two weeks.</p>
<p>The correct approach is <strong>examining CTR and CVR together</strong> to calculate the overall funnel efficiency from click to conversion. If a creative has high CTR but extremely low CVR, there are typically two reasons:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Misaligned hooks</strong>: Clickbait attracts clicks, but the actual product features or pricing differ significantly from user expectations</li>
<li><strong>Broad targeting</strong>: High impressions and clicks, but the landing page attracts non-target users</li>
</ul>
<p>Add a column for "Click-to-Purchase Conversion Rate" in your review spreadsheet — this is the true metric for a creative's monetization capability.</p>
<h2>Dimension 2: Creative Type and Audience Alignment</h2>
<p>Different creative formats naturally suit different audiences. "It runs" is not the standard.</p>
<p><strong>Brand awareness creatives</strong> (strong visuals, fast-paced, high emotional impact) suit interest targeting or broad audiences, focusing on impressions and CTR.</p>
<p><strong>Performance-driven creatives</strong> (product demos, promotions, user testimonials) require precise targeting, measuring CVR and CPA.</p>
<p>If a creative has great click data but poor conversions, first check: Does this creative type match your selected audience? A utility app once used "cool animations + humorous copy" in interest targeting, achieving 4.5% CTR but less than 0.5% signup rate. After switching to "feature demos + use cases," CTR dropped to 2.2% but signup rate jumped to 3.1%.</p>
<p><strong>Quick self-check question</strong>: Is this creative designed to "get clicks" or "drive purchases"? If the answer is unclear, this creative likely falls into the "runs well but doesn't sell" category.</p>
<h2>Dimension 3: Landing Page Consistency</h2>
<p>The most overlooked yet directly impactful factor is consistency between creative and landing page. "Jump breaks" between ad and landing page are a primary cause of high clicks but low conversions.</p>
<p>Common issues include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Creative showcases Product A, landing page shows Product B</li>
<li>Creative displays $29.9, landing page shows $39.9</li>
<li>Creative is in English, landing page is poorly auto-translated Chinese</li>
<li>Creative promises "free trial," landing page immediately requests credit card info</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Practical recommendation</strong>: Before launching any creative, walk through the complete user journey. From ad click → landing page load → above-the-fold content → CTA button, ensure every step maintains consistent messaging and promises. This takes 3 minutes with no technical support needed.</p>
<hr />
<p><strong>Bottom line</strong>: The core purpose of creative review is not finding "runnable creatives" but eliminating "runnable-but-unsellable" ones. Running is a basic threshold; selling is the actual goal. Shift your review metrics from CTR and CPM to CVR and ROAS, and you'll know exactly where your budget is going.</p>
